    "value": "1"
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.amc-10a-2002-p14.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "If the roots are primes \\(p\\) and \\(q,\\) then \\(p+q=63\\) and \\(pq=k.\\) Because \\(63\\) is odd, one prime must be \\(2,\\) making the other \\(61,\\) which is prime.\n\nSo \\(k=2\\cdot 61=122\\) is the only possible value.\n\nThus, the correct answer is B.",
      "hints": [
        "The roots sum to \\(63\\) and multiply to \\(k\\)",
        "An odd sum of two primes forces one of them to be \\(2\\)"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
